//BUG="a recursive call before the function's first ret cannot be normalized"
//OUT="3 2 1 done"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. `normalize_function` only reuses an instance when
// `check_instance_cache` finds one, and `flush_instance_cache` refuses to cache
// an instance whose return type is not known yet. A function's return type is
// pinned by its `ret` statements as the body is normalized, and — for a
// function with no `ret` at all — not until the very last line of
// `normalize_function`:
//
//     if !self.has_ret_statement {
//         self.type_resolver.hint_is(self.curr_func_ret_type, PrimitiveType::Void)?;
//     }
//
// So a recursive call that the normalizer reaches *before* the first `ret`
// always misses the cache and normalizes the function again, until the depth
// hits RECURSION_LIMIT and the compile fails with "This function is in the
// 100-th recursive call in the normalization phase".
//
// A function that returns nothing has no `ret` to put first, so the plain
// shape below cannot be compiled at all. Explicit argument types do not help;
// neither does a trailing bare `ret`, because the recursive call is still
// above it. The workaround is to give the function a base case that returns
// early —
//
//     fn countdown n: i32
//         if n <= 0
//             ret
//         out "{n} "
//         countdown(n - 1)
//
// — which is why every recursion case in 05_functions passes: each one puts
// its base-case `ret` above the recursive call. `fn f n { if n > 0 { x =
// f(n - 1) } ret 0 }` fails the same way with a perfectly ordinary i32 return
// type, so this is about statement order, not about void.
//
// 05_functions/55e is the case the limit exists for: `fn func { ret func() }`
// really has no deducible return type. Here there is nothing to deduce —
// `countdown` is monomorphic and returns void — and the diagnostic tells the
// user to make a return type explicit that the language gives them no way to
// write.
//
// 05_functions/30 establishes that a void function need not return at all, so
// this shape is otherwise legal.

fn countdown n: i32
    if n > 0
        out "{n} "
        countdown(n - 1)

fn main
    countdown(3)
    out "done"
